1
00:00:00,029 --> 00:00:03,250
Open your department’s Weather
Alerts page, then select

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:05,219
Settings beside View History.

3
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:09,470
This training department currently says
there are no active weather alerts.

4
00:00:09,899 --> 00:00:13,539
The Settings page opens Notification
Settings, which describes the

5
00:00:13,539 --> 00:00:17,229
controls for automatically generating
messages to department members.

6
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,540
This lesson prepares and
checks those policy settings.

7
00:00:20,909 --> 00:00:24,040
Practice in a separate training
department before changing how a

8
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:25,870
live team receives information.

9
00:00:26,070 --> 00:00:29,809
Review Enable Weather Alerts
and Minimum Severity to Display.

10
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:33,369
The enable checkbox stays
clear throughout this exercise.

11
00:00:33,829 --> 00:00:36,880
Change the display selection
from Moderate to Severe.

12
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:41,539
The help below says that only alerts
at or above the selected severity

13
00:00:41,569 --> 00:00:42,940
will be shown in the dashboard.

14
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:45,120
This is the display threshold.

15
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:49,309
The separate table below controls
which severities generate automatic

16
00:00:49,309 --> 00:00:53,450
messages, so review both areas when
deciding your department’s policy.

17
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:57,060
Scroll to the complete Auto-Message
Severity Threshold table.

18
00:00:57,510 --> 00:01:03,459
It provides Extreme, Severe, Moderate,
Minor and Unknown rows, each with its own

19
00:01:03,459 --> 00:01:06,600
Enabled checkbox, Start Hour and End Hour.

20
00:01:07,030 --> 00:01:09,299
Every Enabled box remains clear here.

21
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:14,140
The Extreme row contains eighteen
and six: the help explains that this

22
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:18,440
wraps overnight, from six P M to
six A M in department local time.

23
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:22,220
Delivery includes the start
hour and excludes the end hour.

24
00:01:22,569 --> 00:01:25,440
Zero and twenty-four
represent all-day delivery.

25
00:01:25,829 --> 00:01:29,109
Entered hours alone do not
check a row’s Enabled box.

26
00:01:29,259 --> 00:01:32,009
Review Excluded Alert
Events below the table.

27
00:01:32,559 --> 00:01:37,210
The help asks for a comma-separated list
of event titles that should not generate

28
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:41,500
automatic messages, and says those
alerts can still appear in the dashboard.

29
00:01:41,979 --> 00:01:45,950
This example retains only a clearly
fictional Practice alert title.

30
00:01:46,490 --> 00:01:50,439
Treat exclusion as a deliberate
messaging decision, not as a way

31
00:01:50,439 --> 00:01:52,419
to remove an alert from every view.

32
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:55,579
Agree the appropriate event
titles with your department

33
00:01:55,579 --> 00:01:57,570
before changing a live policy.

34
00:01:57,759 --> 00:02:01,810
Below the exclusions, find
Attach Weather Context to Calls.

35
00:02:02,169 --> 00:02:05,900
Its help says that enabling it
attaches active weather alerts near

36
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:07,749
a call’s location as a call note.

37
00:02:08,129 --> 00:02:10,530
Keep it unchecked in
this training exercise.

38
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:14,540
Consider whether that context belongs
in your department’s incident record

39
00:02:14,739 --> 00:02:18,540
and review the resulting notes when
you test an operational configuration.

40
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:22,389
This recording changes no call
notes; it shows the actual

41
00:02:22,389 --> 00:02:24,399
option and its stated purpose.

42
00:02:24,610 --> 00:02:27,290
Select Save Settings
for the prepared policy.

43
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:30,810
Reload Settings and look at
Minimum Severity to Display

44
00:02:30,810 --> 00:02:33,240
again: Severe remains selected.

45
00:02:33,610 --> 00:02:35,919
Enable Weather Alerts is still unchecked.

46
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:39,860
That fresh readback confirms the
threshold was stored beyond the

47
00:02:39,889 --> 00:02:41,879
earlier unsaved dropdown choice.

48
00:02:42,269 --> 00:02:46,180
It does not demonstrate a received
weather alert or a delivered message.

49
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:50,269
Verify the saved controls and any
operational results separately.

50
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:54,390
Return the display selection to
Moderate and select Save Settings again.

51
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:58,320
The page shows Settings saved
successfully beside the button.

52
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:02,559
Reload once more: Moderate is
retained, and global Enable

53
00:03:02,559 --> 00:03:04,099
Weather Alerts remains clear.

54
00:03:04,530 --> 00:03:07,910
Scroll through the full severity
table to confirm its unchecked

55
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:09,490
rows and retained hours.

56
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:12,910
Then review the unchanged
fictional exclusion and

57
00:03:12,959 --> 00:03:14,789
unchecked call-context control.

58
00:03:15,129 --> 00:03:18,400
This leaves the original disabled
training policy in place.

59
00:03:18,519 --> 00:03:22,490
The Alert Sources notice says no
weather alert sources are configured.

60
00:03:22,929 --> 00:03:26,530
Saving notification policy
does not establish a live feed.

61
00:03:26,929 --> 00:03:31,410
Source setup and a verified alert are
separate work before operational use.

62
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:36,389
Search-and-rescue teams, wildland
contractors, industrial emergency teams

63
00:03:36,430 --> 00:03:41,129
and security departments can all benefit
from an agreed weather-information policy,

64
00:03:41,379 --> 00:03:46,340
as can community paramedicine, mental
health response, fire and E M S teams.

65
00:03:46,830 --> 00:03:51,340
Choose the display and messaging behavior
that supports your operations, then

66
00:03:51,470 --> 00:03:53,509
test the complete configured service.

